course: everyday
lesson: 82
title: Getting better, caring for others and medicines
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Whether you have a cold or a fever, your body needs drinks and energy to fight the germs; the old saying «feed a cold, starve a fever» is a myth.
explain: Some children live with a long-term illness like diabetes, epilepsy or asthma; friends who know the signs and fetch an adult can help keep them safe.
explain: Medicine helps only the right person, in the right amount, at the right time; the wrong medicine or too much of it can poison you, so only a trusted adult gives it.
explain: A boy in Dallas finds colourful pills on the kitchen table; he does not touch them, he tells his dad, and his dad puts them away up high.
fact: With a cold or a fever, eat what you can and drink plenty, because the body needs fluids and energy in both.
fact: When you visit someone who is ill, wash your hands and keep your distance if they are infectious.
fact: A classmate who is often away ill needs kindness and help to catch up.
fact: If a friend with diabetes looks shaky, pale and confused, tell an adult at once, because their blood sugar may be too low and they may need sugar quickly.
fact: Children do not get diabetes from eating too many sweets; it is nobody's fault.
fact: Never take medicine unless a trusted adult gives it to you.
fact: Never take someone else's medicine, because it was meant for their body and their illness.
fact: Take a course of antibiotics to the end as the doctor says, even when you feel better, or the infection can come back.
fact: Aspirin should not be given to children under 16 unless a doctor says so, because it can cause a rare but serious illness called Reye's syndrome.
fact: If you swallow medicine or something poisonous by mistake, tell an adult immediately so they can call poison control or emergency help.
fact: Cleaning products are poisonous and can burn, so never drink them or play with them.
exercise: Which is true about the saying «feed a cold, starve a fever»? | It is a myth; you need food and drink with both | It is true; you should not eat at all while you have a fever | It is true; you should eat double with a cold | It only works for children, not adults
exercise: You visit your grandma who has the flu. What should you do? | Wash your hands and keep a little distance | Hug her tightly and share her cup so she feels less alone | Stay away from her for a whole year | Bring her a cold glass of cola
exercise: A classmate is often away ill. How can you help? | Be kind and help them catch up | Tell everyone they are just lazy | Leave them out of your games at break | Ask them to stop being ill
exercise: Your friend has epilepsy. What is a good thing to do? | Learn what to do and tell an adult if they seem unwell | Never play with them at all in case they suddenly have a seizure | Tell other children to keep away | Pretend you do not know about it
exercise: A friend with diabetes suddenly looks shaky, pale and confused. What do you do? | Tell an adult at once; they may need sugar | Let them sleep it off in a quiet corner | Give them their insulin injection yourself | Wait until the end of the lesson
exercise: You find some pills on the table. What should you do? | Leave them and tell a trusted adult | Taste one quickly to find out what it is | Put them in your pocket for later | Share them out with your sister
exercise: Your friend offers you some of his cough medicine. What do you say? | No, I never take someone else's medicine | Yes, if I have a cough too | Yes, but only half a spoon | Yes, if it tastes of strawberries or cherries
exercise: Why should you finish a course of antibiotics? | Stopping early can let the infection come back | The bottle must be empty before it can be recycled | Antibiotics only start working at the end | The doctor will be angry if pills are left
exercise: Why should children not be given aspirin unless a doctor says? | It can cause a rare but serious illness | It does not work on children's headaches | It makes children grow too fast | It is too bitter for children to swallow
exercise: Your little brother swallowed some of mum's pills by mistake. What do you do? | Tell an adult immediately so they can get help | Give him a big glass of milk and wait | Make him run around fast to use the pills up | Keep it secret so that he does not get into any trouble
exercise: Why should you never play with bottles from under the sink? | Cleaning products are poisonous and can burn | They are too heavy for a child to lift up safely | They make the floor slippery | They cost a lot of money to replace
exercise: Why should a medicine be taken only by the person it was given to? | It was chosen for their body and their illness | Medicines lose all their power as soon as they are shared | Other people's medicine always tastes bad | Each bottle has a name that must match
test: A girl in Leeds feels better after three days of antibiotics but has four days left. What should she do? | Keep taking them as the doctor said | Stop, because she feels better now | Give the rest to her brother | Save the rest for the next time she gets ill
test: An auntie wants to give a 10-year-old aspirin for a headache. What is true? | Aspirin is not for under-16s unless a doctor says | Aspirin is the best and safest medicine for children | Aspirin is fine if you take two tablets | Aspirin only matters for babies
test: A boy has a fever and does not feel like eating much. What should he do? | Eat what he can and drink plenty | Eat nothing until the fever is gone | Drink nothing so the fever dries out | Eat as much as possible to win
test: Which of these is the safest rule about medicine? | Only take medicine a trusted adult gives you | Take medicine if the box looks friendly | Take medicine if a friend says it helped them | Take medicine whenever you feel tired or a bit low
test: A classmate with diabetes says she feels wobbly and strange at break. What should happen first? | A teacher should be told straight away | She should go for a run to wake herself up | She should skip lunch to feel lighter | Her friends should wait and watch
test: Why is it good to learn what to do if a friend has a long-term illness? | You can help keep them safe | You can tell them what to eat every day | You can take their medicine for them | You can explain their illness to strangers
test: A toddler in Brisbane drank from a bottle of bleach. What is the right action? | Tell an adult at once so they call for help | Wait for a while to see if the toddler feels sick | Give the toddler salt water to drink | Put the bottle back and say nothing
test: Why do we keep our distance from a friend with a bad cold? | Germs can spread from them to us | Colds make people grumpy | It is rude to stand close to people | Colds are caught by looking at someone
test: found some pills in mums bag, prob vitamins, can i have one | No, only take medicine a trusted adult gives you | Yes, vitamins are just like sweets, they can't hurt | Yes, if you take just one | Yes, if they taste nice
test: feel better now, can i stop the antibiotics, still 3 days left | No, finish the course as the doctor says | Yes, stop as soon as you feel better | Yes, save the rest for next time | Yes, if the fever is gone
test: my mate with diabetes is shaky n pale n confused | Tell an adult at once, she may need sugar quickly | Let her sleep it off quietly | Give her a big glass of water | Tell her to run around the field to warm herself up
test: lil sis drank some of the cleaning spray!! | Tell an adult now so they call poison help | Give her milk and wait | Make her sleep it off | Do nothing if she seems fine
explain: A boy in Ohio stopped his antibiotics after two days because he felt better; the ear infection came back worse and he needed a second, longer course.
explain: A girl in Brisbane thought two of her mum's headache tablets would work faster than one of hers; she got very ill and had to be taken to hospital.
fact: Taking more of a medicine than the dose on the label can poison you, even with common medicines like paracetamol.
fact: Medicines are made for one person and one illness, so taking someone else's can be dangerous.
test: feel better already, can i stop the antibiotics | Ask a parent, you usually finish the whole course | Yes, stop the moment you feel ok | Take them all at once to finish quick | Give the rest to your brother
test: headache, mum's pills r right there, 2 should do it | No, ask an adult, the wrong medicine or dose can poison you | Yes, two always works faster | Yes, adult pills are the same | Take three to be safe
test: sore throat, can i have my sister's medicine from last week | No, medicine is only for the person it was given to | Yes, all medicine works for everyone | Yes, if it's the same colour | Only if you take double
test: heard a girl took a whole bunch of her mum's tablets to get better faster and ended up in hospital, what's the real lesson | Only take medicine a trusted adult gives you, in the right dose | Mum's tablets are fine, just take fewer | Tablets only work if you take lots at once | Medicine is safe if it is in the kitchen
test: my uncle says "feed a cold, starve a fever" so i shouldn't eat with my temp, true? | A myth; with a cold or fever you eat what you can and drink plenty | True, eating makes a fever much worse | True, but you must not drink anything either | True, starve both a cold and a fever
test: everyone's saying Ella has diabetes cos she ate too many sweets, should i tell people | No, that's untrue gossip; be kind and know how to help her | Yes, everyone should know it is her fault | Yes, but only tell people who sit near her | Yes, and stop her from ever eating at school
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a course of antibiotics is completed as the doctor prescribes. | Otherwise the infection may return | Otherwise the tablets will be wasted | Otherwise the doctor will be offended | Otherwise the medicine loses its colour
exam: Whereas a friend with diabetes appears shaky, pale and confused, determine the appropriate course of action. | Inform an adult at once, as the blood sugar may be too low | Allow the friend to sleep it off without help | Give the friend some of one's own medicine | Wait until the end of the school day to mention it
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the use of aspirin in children under sixteen years of age? | It is not given unless a doctor so directs | It is the preferred remedy for all childhood pain | It may be given freely in any quantity | It is permitted provided it is taken with milk
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the medicine of another person. | It must not be taken, as it was meant for their body and illness | It may be taken if one's symptoms appear similar | It may be taken if the person gives permission | It may be taken provided the dose is halved
